==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
From the {{der|en|la|īnstīgātus}}, past participle of {{m|la|instigo|īnstīgāre|t=to instigate}}, from prefix {{m|la|in-|t=in}} + {{m|la||*stigare}}, akin to {{m|la|stinguo|stinguere|t=push, goad}}. Akin to {{cog|de|stechen|t=to prick}}, {{cog|en|stick}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈɪnstəɡeɪt/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-instigate.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|instigat}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} to [[incite]]; to bring about by urging or encouraging
#: {{ux|en|to '''instigate''' a riot}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} to [[goad]] or [[urge]] (a person) forward, especially to wicked actions; to [[provoke]]
#: {{ux|en|to '''instigate''' someone to a crime}}
#* {{rfdatek|lang=en|Bishop Warburton}}
#*: He hath only '''instigated''' his blackest agents to the very extent of their malignity.
#: {{syn|en|animate|encourage|impel|incite|provoke|spur|stimulate|tempt|urge}}
#: {{ant|en|halt|prevent|stop}}

